static const WorldBody WORLD_BODIES[] = {
    {"events/ames_survey.txt", R"WCSV(Peter Ames, a young army surveyor, has pinned a map of the valley to a barrel. The river is drawn; the rest is white. 'Camp Collins needs a name for every ford and bend,' he says. 'I'll write down whatever people tell me.'
)WCSV"},
    {"events/canyon_willow.txt", R"WCSV(Little Willow stands knee-deep at the canyon mouth, reading the current like a page. She guides families across for trade goods and has no patience for people who guess. 'The river is higher than it looks,' she says. 'It always is, this month.'
)WCSV"},
    {"events/finale_mason.txt", R"WCSV(Mason has been here first, naturally. His line is already on Ames's map: straight, tidy, and ruled across ground that has been used for a very long time. 'He's very persuasive,' Ames says. 'And it's very clear.'
)WCSV"},
    {"events/finale_names.txt", R"WCSV(Ames lifts his pencil. You carry names now: the ones Friday gave, the ones Willow taught, the ones Janis kept. 'They won't all fit,' he says. 'Some won't mean anything to the men who read this.' The paper is waiting.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/finale_plain.txt", R"WCSV(Camp Collins is gone from Laporte; the army will rebuild on higher ground, and Ames is already staking the new lines. He asks, a little shyly, what you saw on the road: any marks, any crossings, any names. You saw a good deal. You have not decided what it is for.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/friday_counsel.txt", R"WCSV(Chief Friday meets you beneath the cottonwood. He does not offer folklore. He offers calibration: how to move without mistaking speed for wisdom, how to hear warning before it becomes consequence, how to stay visible without becoming reckless.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/friday_river.txt", R"WCSV(Friday walks the bend with you and names it: where the channel ran before the last flood, where families camp in spring, where the cottonwoods drown first. 'The river moves,' he says. 'The map says it does not. One of those will be wrong.'
)WCSV"},
    {"events/high_water.txt", R"WCSV(Two nights of rain on the range, and the river came down in the dark. Cottonwoods go by, then a barn door, then something that was a wagon. The camp at the bend is under water and people are shouting names.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/janis_store.txt", R"WCSV(The store smells of harness leather, coffee, wet wool and river mud. Antoine Janis has traded here since 1858. He speaks plainly, like a man who knows this valley had owners, obligations and names before anyone's map.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/mason_point.txt", R"WCSV(Mason notices the lump in your coat. 'Is that a point?' He turns it over with real tenderness and a price already forming. 'There's a man in Denver who pays for these. Eight dollars in goods, today.'
)WCSV"},
    {"events/mason_reveal.txt", R"WCSV(Mason receives you on the porch of a cabin with no roof yet. He knows exactly what Laporte is and exactly what is replacing it, and he does not need to hate the old order to leave it behind. He only needs to know which papers, contracts and roads are about to matter.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/mason_witness.txt", R"WCSV(Mason produces a claim paper with your name already on the witness line and one plain sentence above it: the land described is vacant and unoccupied. He has a pen, and the courtesy not to rush you.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/old_point.txt", R"WCSV(In a washout behind the station, rain has uncovered a leaf-shaped stone point as long as your finger, a channel flaked down each face. Whoever made it hunted here long before the stage road, the army, or any name for this river.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/trail_antelope.txt", R"WCSV(A small band of pronghorn stands on the ridge, watching you with perfect disinterest.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/trail_fever.txt", R"WCSV(A child in the party burns with fever, and nothing in the wagon seems to help except time and the little brown bottle.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/trail_ford.txt", R"WCSV(The ford ahead is wider than it was yesterday. A freight wagon sits on the far bank, its driver shouting something the water swallows.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/trail_patrol.txt", R"WCSV(Four mounted soldiers stop you. The sergeant, polite and tired, wants to know which camps you have passed, who was in them, and what they said.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/trail_stage.txt", R"WCSV(The Overland stage thunders past in a banner of dust, six horses at a dead run. The driver flings a folded newspaper at your feet.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/trail_storm.txt", R"WCSV(A line of black cloud walks down off the range. Hail the size of walnuts starts before you can finish unhitching.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/trail_traders.txt", R"WCSV(An Arapaho family bound for the summer hunting grounds draws alongside, children walking, horses loaded high. They trade as they travel, courteous and appraising.
)WCSV"},
    {"events/virginia_dale_station.txt", R"WCSV(Virginia Slade runs the home station on labor, memory and nerves held in check. Word has come from Virginia City that Jack Slade is dead, hanged by vigilantes, and she has not said so aloud. The horses here are better cared for than most people on the road.
)WCSV"},
};
